      "en": "New Brighton was founded in the 1830s by James Atherton, who envisioned a premier seaside resort for the elite of Liverpool. Perched on the north-eastern tip of the Wirral Peninsula, the town commands impressive views across the Mersey Estuary. Its historical landmarks include the formidable Fort Perch Rock, a sandstone battery completed in 1829, and the iconic Perch Rock Lighthouse, which stood guard over the shipping channels for over a century. During the Victorian and Edwardian eras, it was a bustling leisure hub, home to one of the UK's tallest towers and largest open-air swimming pools, though these have since vanished. Today, the town has seen a massive regeneration, particularly around the Marine Point area, which features a cinema, theatre, and numerous dining options. The long promenade remains a staple for locals and visitors alike, offering a refreshing escape with a blend of historical charm and contemporary seaside attractions."
