      "en": "Ordsall is a historic neighborhood in Salford, Greater Manchester, occupying a prime position on the banks of the River Irwell. The area is most famous for Ordsall Hall, a magnificent Grade I listed Tudor manor house that dates back to the 15th century and serves as a vital cultural anchor for the community. For centuries, Ordsall was a rural estate until the Industrial Revolution transformed it into a densely populated industrial heartland. The development of the Salford Docks and the opening of the Manchester Ship Canal in 1894 turned Ordsall into a focal point of global trade, with its streets lined with terraced housing for dock workers. However, the decline of the shipping industry in the late 20th century led to significant economic challenges. In recent decades, Ordsall has undergone extensive urban regeneration, evolving into a desirable residential district that bridges the gap between Salford’s historic past and its high-tech future. It is now adjacent to the world-class MediaCityUK and Salford Quays developments, attracting professionals in the media and creative industries. The area features a mix of beautifully restored historic buildings and contemporary waterfront apartments, supported by a growing service economy and excellent light rail links to Manchester city centre."
