      "en": "Wombourne is a large, historic village and civil parish located in the South Staffordshire district, near the border with the West Midlands. It is widely renowned for its unique status, often claiming to be the largest village in England based on its administrative structure and community identity. The village is defined by its scenic layout, featuring a traditional village green surrounded by local businesses and historic inns. A key landmark is the Staffordshire and Worcestershire Canal, which hosts the remarkable Bratch Locks—a historic flight of three locks that were converted from a staircase in the 19th century and feature a unique pumping station. The local economy, once rooted in iron smelting and agriculture, has evolved into a thriving service and residential hub. The village is also a gateway to the South Staffordshire Railway Walk, a popular nature trail that follows the path of the old Wombourne branch line, providing exceptional views of the Staffordshire countryside."
