      "en": "Bitterne Park is a thriving residential suburb of Southampton, characterized by its elevated position overlooking the River Itchen. The development of the area began in earnest in 1882 when the National Liberal Land Company purchased the Bitterne Manor estate for housing. The suburb was designed with a focus on green spaces and suburban comfort, leading to the creation of the 'Triangle' – a focal point for local commerce and community life. The most significant geographical feature is the River Itchen, which forms the western boundary. Along its banks lies Riverside Park, covering approximately 32 hectares of open space, including sports pitches, a miniature railway, and walking paths. The architecture is predominantly late Victorian and Edwardian, with many houses featuring original period details. The area is connected to the rest of the city via Cobden Bridge, which was first built in 1883. Bitterne Park remains a popular choice for families due to its excellent schools and abundance of parks."
