      "en": "Bishopwearmouth is one of the three historic parishes that consolidated to form the city of Sunderland. Its history began in 930 AD when King Athelstan granted the lands to the Bishop of Durham, making it an important ecclesiastical center. For centuries, it remained a distinct village until the industrial boom of the 18th century integrated it into the growing urban sprawl of Sunderland. The focal point is the Sunderland Minster, formerly St Michael's Church, which has stood in various forms since the Anglo-Saxon period. The district is also home to the Sunderland Empire Theatre, a grand Edwardian venue opened in 1907 that remains one of the premier theaters in the UK. Bishopwearmouth was the administrative heart of the borough, housing the Town Hall and other civic buildings during the Victorian era. Today, the area is part of the city's 'Culture Quarter', featuring Mowbray Park, the Sunderland Museum and Winter Gardens, and many historic pubs and commercial buildings that reflect its long evolution from a bishop's manor to a modern city center."
