      "en": "Halliwell is a historic district located to the north of Bolton town centre in Greater Manchester. Its development is a classic story of the Industrial Revolution, where it evolved from a collection of farms into a major industrial suburb. The area was once famous for its massive cotton mills, such as the Dove Mill, which defined the economic and social life of the residents. The name Halliwell is believed to mean 'Holy Well', referring to a local spring that was historically thought to have healing properties. Adjacent to Halliwell is the historic Smithills Hall, a Grade I listed manor house that dates back to the 14th century, offering a sharp contrast to the surrounding industrial architecture. Today, Halliwell is home to a diverse population of approximately 13,929 people. While the chimneys no longer smoke, the legacy of the cotton trade is etched into the landscape of red-brick terraces. The area is now a key residential hub, with Halliwell Road providing a busy corridor of local shops, services, and community life."
