      "en": "Canonbury is an affluent and historically significant residential district in the London Borough of Islington. Originally a rural manor owned by the Canons of St Bartholomew's Priory, it has retained a distinct character characterized by its Tudor and Georgian architecture. The defining landmark is the Canonbury Tower, constructed in the 1590s as part of a grand manor house; it has hosted illustrious figures such as Sir Francis Bacon and Oliver Goldsmith. The area saw major development in the early 19th century when elegant squares like Canonbury Square were laid out, featuring the classic brick terraces that London is famous for. Canonbury has a prestigious literary reputation, most notably as the residence of George Orwell during the 1940s. The New River Walk, a linear park following the course of a 17th-century aqueduct, provides a scenic and tranquil escape for residents. Protected as a conservation area, Canonbury remains a peaceful enclave that offers a high quality of life just a short distance from the financial heart of London."
