      "en": "Bargoed is a prominent settlement in the Rhymney Valley of South Wales, fundamentally shaped by the industrial revolution and the subsequent rise of the coal industry. The town's modern identity was forged in 1897 with the sinking of the Bargoed Colliery, which for decades was a primary employer and a source of world-renowned steam coal. Geographically, the town is characterized by its valley-side setting, with steep streets that lead down to the banks of the Rhymney River. Since the closure of the heavy industries, Bargoed has benefited from significant investment through the Greater Bargoed project, which aimed to revitalize the retail sector and improve accessibility. Culturally, the town remains a stronghold of Welsh community values, with a long-standing tradition in male voice choirs and a fierce passion for rugby union, reflected in the local club's prominence in the community's social life."
