      "en": "Castle Bromwich is a historic settlement in the West Midlands with a legacy that spans over a millennium. Originally a small village, it expanded rapidly in the 20th century while preserving its historical core. The most significant landmark is Castle Bromwich Hall, a beautiful 17th-century Jacobean mansion set within world-renowned formal gardens. These gardens are one of the best-preserved examples of an early 18th-century English landscape. Beyond its manor house, Castle Bromwich is famous for its massive contribution to British aviation. The Castle Bromwich Aircraft Factory was the largest of its kind, producing over half of all Spitfires built during World War II, as well as many Lancaster bombers. Today, the area is a thriving residential community within the Borough of Solihull, known for its good schools and local heritage projects. The nearby River Tame and the surrounding parklands provide essential green space for the urban population, blending the industrial past with a tranquil present."
