      "en": "Haydock is a large village and industrial settlement in the Metropolitan Borough of St Helens, Merseyside. Its development was historically driven by the massive expansion of the South Lancashire coalfield during the Industrial Revolution. The Haydock Collieries, owned by Richard Evans & Co., were among the largest and most advanced in Britain, turning the area into a booming industrial hub. Tragic mining accidents, such as the Wood Pit explosion of 1878, left a deep mark on the local community and are still remembered today. In contrast to its heavy industrial past, Haydock is internationally renowned for the Haydock Park Racecourse. Established in 1899 on the parkland of the former Haydock Lodge, it is one of the premier venues for horse racing in the UK, hosting major Group 1 races. Geographically, Haydock sits on a flat coastal plain, which has facilitated its transition from coal mining to a major regional logistics center. The strategic intersection of the M6 motorway and the East Lancashire Road makes it a vital transport node. Despite extensive industrial development, the town maintains a strong community identity and features several public parks and recreational areas."
