    "descriptionAdvanced": {
      "de": "Pallion ist ein traditionsreicher Stadtteil von Sunderland im Nordosten Englands, gelegen am Südufer des Flusses Wear. Historisch gesehen war Pallion das Herz der industriellen Revolution in der Region, insbesondere durch seine weltberühmten Schiffswerften wie Doxford & Sons. Hier wurden einige der innovativsten Schiffe ihrer Zeit gebaut, was Sunderland einst zur 'größten Schiffbaustadt der Welt' machte. Nach dem Niedergang der Schwerindustrie in den 1980er Jahren vollzog das Viertel einen massiven Strukturwandel. Heute ist Pallion ein wichtiges wirtschaftliches Standbein Sunderlands, geprägt durch große Einzelhandelsparks und moderne Gewerbegebiete. Die Eröffnung der Northern Spire Bridge im Jahr 2018 hat die Anbindung des Viertels erheblich verbessert. Trotz der Modernisierung sind viele Spuren der industriellen Vergangenheit, wie alte Dockanlagen und Arbeiterhäuser, noch im Stadtbild präsent. Die Gemeinschaft ist stark durch ihre Arbeitergeschichte geprägt und blickt stolz auf ihre maritime Tradition zurück.",
      "hu": "Pallion Sunderland egyik legfontosabb ipari és lakónegyede, amely a Wear-folyó déli partján helyezkedik el Tyne and Wear megyében. A negyed neve valószínűleg a 'pavilon' szóból ered, utalva egy egykori udvarházra. A 19. és 20. században Pallion a világ egyik legjelentősebb hajógyártó központja volt; itt működött a William Doxford & Sons hajógyár, amely forradalmasította a tengeri szállítást. A hajógyárak bezárása után a terület jelentős átalakuláson ment keresztül, és ma már Sunderland egyik legnagyobb kereskedelmi zónája, ahol számos áruház és logisztikai központ kapott helyet. A 2018-ban átadott Northern Spire híd közvetlenül Pallion mellett köti össze a folyó két partját, modern arculatot adva a környéknek. A lakóövezetben a hagyományos vörös téglás sorházak dominálnak, amelyek a gyári munkások számára épültek a múlt század elején. Pallion ma a megújulás és az ipari örökség megőrzésének egyik példája az északkelet-angliai régióban.",
      "ro": "Pallion este un district situat în orașul Sunderland, pe malul sudic al râului Wear, având o identitate strâns legată de trecutul său industrial glorios. Timp de peste un secol, Pallion a fost epicentrul construcțiilor navale globale, găzduind șantierul naval Doxford, renumit pentru inovațiile sale în proiectarea navelor comerciale. Decăderea industriei grele în a doua jumătate a secolului XX a forțat localitatea să se reinventeze, transformându-se dintr-un centru de producție într-unul de retail și servicii. Astăzi, Pallion Retail Park este una dintre cele mai mari zone comerciale din regiune, atrăgând vizitatori din tot Sunderland-ul. Din punct de vedere geografic, zona este marcată de relieful abrupt către malul râului și de prezența Podului Northern Spire, o structură arhitecturală modernă care a revitalizat legăturile de transport. Deși modernizat, cartierul păstrează rândurile de case victoriene construite inițial pentru muncitori, oferind o privire asupra istoriei sociale a Nord-Estului Angliei.",
      "en": "Pallion is a key industrial and residential district of Sunderland, Tyne and Wear, located on the southern bank of the River Wear. The area's history is fundamentally defined by the shipbuilding industry, most notably the William Doxford & Sons shipyard, which at its peak was one of the most productive in the world. Doxford's developed several innovative ship designs here, including the 'turret deck' ship. Following the decline of heavy industry in the late 20th century, Pallion underwent significant regeneration, transitioning into a major commercial and retail hub for the city. It is home to extensive retail parks and business estates that have replaced the former shipyard sites. The landscape was further transformed by the 2018 opening of the Northern Spire bridge, a landmark structure that connects Pallion to the north side of the river. Despite these changes, the community remains rooted in its working-class heritage, with many original Edwardian terraced houses still standing. Today, Pallion serves as a vital economic engine for Sunderland, blending industrial history with modern infrastructure."
    },
