      "en": "Bowdon is a historic village and highly affluent suburb located on a sandstone ridge overlooking the Cheshire Plain in Greater Manchester. Its history stretches back to the Anglo-Saxon era, with a significant entry in the 1086 Domesday Book. The area underwent a dramatic transformation during the Victorian era when it became a preferred retreat for Manchester's wealthy textile magnates and merchants. These 'Cotton Kings' built grand villas in a variety of revivalist styles, creating the leafy, upscale character that defines Bowdon today. The village is centered around the Grade II* listed St Mary's Church, which occupies a prominent hilltop site. Geographically, Bowdon provides a physical barrier between the urban sprawl of Manchester and the rural landscapes of Cheshire. Today, it remains one of the most prestigious postcodes in Northern England, known for its high property values, elite schools, and quiet, tree-lined avenues. While largely residential, its proximity to Altrincham's commercial center ensures it remains a vital, if secluded, part of the wider metropolitan area."
