      "en": "Pelaw is a distinct district in the Metropolitan Borough of Gateshead, renowned for its transformation from an industrial powerhouse to a modern residential suburb. The area's history was fundamentally shaped by the Co-operative Wholesale Society (CWS), which established a major manufacturing base here starting in 1902. This 'Co-op Town' produced everything from shirts and cabinets to the world-famous Pelaw Polish, exporting goods across the globe. The brick-built factories were masterpieces of industrial design and created a self-contained community for the workforce. In the 21st century, Pelaw has reinvented itself; while most manufacturing has ceased, the architectural legacy continues through successful residential redevelopments of the former CWS buildings. Its geography as a junction point remains its greatest asset, with Pelaw Metro station acting as the vital split in the network for trains heading towards the coast or the city, making it a highly connected and desirable place to live."
