      "en": "Stansted Mountfitchet is a large, affluent village in the Uttlesford district of Essex, situated near the borders with Hertfordshire. Its dual name combines the Old English 'Stansted' (stony place) with the surname of the Norman Mountfitchet family, who constructed a significant castle here following the Norman Conquest. The site of this fortress now hosts Mountfitchet Castle, a renowned open-air museum featuring a full-scale wooden reconstruction of a Norman motte-and-bailey castle, complete with livestock and period-accurate buildings. The village is also home to a fine brick tower windmill, built in 1787 and Grade II* listed, which serves as a landmark for miles around. Perhaps most significantly in the modern era, the village is located just a few miles from London Stansted Airport, the UK's fourth busiest airport, which has brought significant infrastructure and employment to the area. Additionally, the House on the Hill Toy Museum offers a vast collection of memorabilia, claims to be the largest in Europe. With its medieval roots and strategic position on the rail line between London Liverpool Street and Cambridge, Stansted Mountfitchet remains a highly desirable place to live and visit."
