      "en": "East Leake is a substantial village and civil parish located in the Rushcliffe district of Nottinghamshire. Recorded in the Domesday Book as 'Leche', the settlement has evolved from a small agricultural hamlet into a thriving community. The dominant feature of the local economy is the gypsum industry; East Leake is the global headquarters for British Gypsum, which operates a large manufacturing site and a training academy here. The parish church, dedicated to St. Mary, is a Grade I listed building featuring a unique 13th-century 'shawm' (an early musical instrument) and a nave that dates back to the late 11th century. The village is also geographically significant as it sits on the route of the Great Central Railway, the UK's only double-track main line heritage railway. Despite its industrial importance, East Leake retains a strong rural identity, surrounded by the rolling hills of the Nottinghamshire Wolds and offering a high quality of life with its own secondary school and multiple local amenities."
