      "en": "Ottery St Mary is a historic market town in East Devon, celebrated for its rich cultural heritage and dramatic traditions. It is most famous for the annual Tar Barrels festival held on November 5th, where residents carry flaming, tar-soaked barrels on their shoulders through the streets, a tradition dating back centuries. The town is also the birthplace of the great Romantic poet Samuel Taylor Coleridge, born here in 1772; his legacy is honored throughout the town. Architecturally, the town is dominated by the Church of St Mary, a stunning 14th-century structure that serves as a miniature version of Exeter Cathedral, complete with matching transept towers. Set in the lush Otter Valley, the town historically thrived on the wool trade and lacemaking. Today, it remains a vibrant community with a mix of independent retailers and historic buildings. Nearby attractions like Escot Park and the Jurassic Coast make it a popular base for exploring the natural beauty of Devon."
