      "en": "Newbold is a historic and populous suburb of Chesterfield in Derbyshire, England, located to the north and west of the town center. Its name is derived from the Old English 'Niwubold', meaning 'new building', and it was a significant manor at the time of the Domesday Book in 1086. Historically, Newbold was far more extensive than the town of Chesterfield itself, which was originally a smaller settlement within Newbold's parish. The area's development was propelled by the Industrial Revolution, utilizing rich local deposits of coal and ironstone. It became particularly renowned for its pottery industry, producing durable stoneware that was traded widely across the Midlands. A key historical landmark is the 13th-century Eyre Chapel, a Grade II* listed building that served the Eyre family and remains one of the oldest places of worship in the district. Geographically, Newbold occupies an elevated position, providing a gateway to the nearby Peak District National Park. In the modern era, Newbold has transitioned into a key residential area, featuring a mix of traditional terrace housing and expansive post-war estates, while maintaining green lungs like the Queen's Park and the Holmebrook Valley Park on its fringes."
