      "en": "Stepps is a well-established residential community on the northeastern outskirts of Glasgow, within the North Lanarkshire council area. Originally known as 'Stepps of Road', its name reflects its historical location on a vital thoroughfare through the marshy terrain of central Scotland. The 19th century marked a period of significant growth for the village, driven by the textile bleaching industry and its proximity to the coal mines of Lanarkshire. The arrival of the railway in the mid-1800s further catalyzed its development, shifting its focus towards becoming a premier commuter suburb. Today, Stepps is characterized by a mix of traditional stone-built houses and contemporary residential estates. It is bordered by Frankfield Loch, a vital local biodiversity site. The town's strategic position near the M80 motorway and its efficient rail links ensure it remains one of the most accessible and desirable places to live in the Greater Glasgow area."
