      "en": "Littlemore is a village and civil parish located on the southeastern edge of Oxford, England. It holds a unique place in English ecclesiastical history due to its strong association with John Henry Newman, a leading figure in the Oxford Movement. In the early 1840s, Newman established a semi-monastic community here, transforming the village into a pilgrimage site for those interested in religious reform. He was instrumental in building the parish church and the local school, which still serve the community today. During the Victorian era and the 20th century, Littlemore expanded significantly beyond its rural roots. The former Littlemore Hospital, once a sprawling asylum, played a major role in the local economy for over a century before its closure in the late 1990s. The site has since been redeveloped into a blend of high-tech office spaces and residential areas. Currently, Littlemore is home to the Oxford Science Park, a major center for innovation and bioscience. Despite its modern industrial and residential growth, the historic core of the village remains distinct, characterized by traditional stone cottages and a strong sense of community."
